\title{The Ordered Field of Exponential--Logarithmic Transseries:\\
Structure, Faithfulness, and the Reduction of Real Closedness}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{16 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop the theory of an explicit ordered field $\T$ of \emph{exponential--logarithmic
(EML) transseries}: Hahn series whose value group is the four-dimensional
lexicographically ordered group $\R^4$, whose coordinates record the four scales of the
growth hierarchy $e^{e^x}\gg e^x\gg x\gg\log x$. We prove that the transmonomials form a
multiplicative group isomorphic to $(\R^4,+)$ whose asymptotic ordering is exactly the
lexicographic ordering of exponent data; that $\T$ is a non-Archimedean ordered field in
which every positive element has $n$-th roots of all orders, so that the nonnegative
elements are precisely the squares and $\T$ is Euclidean and formally real; and that
consequently the ordering is definable in the ring language, is the unique compatible
ordering, and is preserved by every ring homomorphism out of $\T$.

We then connect $\T$ to analysis. The algebra $\mathcal{E}$ of finite real linear
combinations of transmonomials maps into $\T$ by formal expansion and into the ring of
germs at $+\infty$ by evaluation; both maps are injective ring homomorphisms with
identical fibres, and the correspondence is an order isomorphism onto its image. The key
result is the \textbf{Asymptotic Comparison Theorem} in two forms: a transseries dominated
by every transmonomial is zero, and an EML function that is $o(\mm)$ for every
transmonomial $\mm$ is zero. Thus the EML scale admits no flat germs and the transseries
expansion is a complete invariant. We equip $\mathcal{E}$ with a derivation computing the
analytic derivative, identify its kernel as exactly $\R$, prove that EML germs form a
Hardy field, and establish a Liouville-type obstruction: $1/(x\log x)$ has no
antiderivative in $\mathcal{E}$, because $\log\log x$ is flat against the entire EML scale.

Finally we attack real closedness of $\T$. We reduce it to the odd-degree root property
for monic polynomials, exhibit roots not obtainable by radicals via Henselian lifting (the
\emph{casus irreducibilis} cubic $z^3-3z+t$ for infinitesimal $t$), and prove a Newton
scaling theorem normalising an arbitrary monic polynomial together with a Cauchy root
bound, sharpening the reduction to \emph{normalised} monic odd-degree polynomials. Full
real closedness of $\T$ remains open; we delimit the gap precisely.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords.} transseries, Hahn series, Hardy field, asymptotic expansion,
real closed field, Newton polygon, Hensel's lemma, exp-log functions.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem with power series at infinity}

Asymptotic analysis at $+\infty$ has always suffered from an expressivity gap. A power
series in $1/x$ can describe the behaviour of $x/(x+1)$ or of $e^{1/x}$, but it cannot
describe $e^{x}$, $\log x$, or $x^{\pi}$. Worse, it cannot \emph{distinguish}: the
classical obstruction is the existence of \textbf{flat} functions, nonzero functions all
of whose asymptotic coefficients vanish. The standard example $e^{-1/t^{2}}$ at $t=0$
translates at infinity into $e^{-x}$, which is $o(x^{-n})$ for every $n$ and therefore
invisible to any expansion in powers of $1/x$. Asymptotic expansion in a fixed scale is
thus not injective, and no amount of care recovers a function from its expansion.

Transseries fix the gap by enlarging the scale until it is \emph{closed} under the
operations one cares about, and then proving that in the enlarged scale nothing is flat.
This paper develops in full a concrete four-scale slice of that theory: explicit and
computable, yet carrying all the structural theorems.

\subsection{What is proved here}

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item \textbf{The ordered field.} Construction of $\T$, the group structure of
  transmonomials, the Scale Comparison Theorem, the strict growth hierarchy, and
  non-Archimedeanity.
\item \textbf{Roots and order rigidity.} $n$-th roots of positive elements; squares
  $=$ nonnegatives; formal reality; definability, uniqueness and rigidity of the ordering;
  quadratic solvability.
\item \textbf{Faithfulness.} Two injective ring homomorphisms out of the EML algebra, the
  order embedding, and the Asymptotic Comparison Theorem in formal and analytic form.
\item \textbf{Differential structure.} A derivation compatible with real differentiation;
  the constants theorem; Hardy-field behaviour; the Liouville-type obstruction.
\item \textbf{Towards real closedness.} Reduction to odd-degree monic polynomials,
  Henselian roots beyond radicals, and Newton scaling normalisation.
\end{enumerate}

Throughout, ``eventually'' means ``for all sufficiently large $x$'' and $f\prec g$ means
$f=o(g)$ as $x\to+\infty$.

\section{The rank group and the field of transseries}

\subsection{Growth ranks}

\begin{definition}[Rank group]
The \emph{EML rank group} is
\[
\Gamma \;=\; \R\times_{\mathrm{lex}}\bigl(\R\times_{\mathrm{lex}}(\R\times_{\mathrm{lex}}\R)\bigr),
\]
the additive group $\R^{4}$ with the lexicographic order: $(d,a,b,c)<(d',a',b',c')$ iff
$d<d'$, or $d=d'\wedge a<a'$, or $d=d'\wedge a=a'\wedge b<b'$, or $d=d'\wedge a=a'\wedge
b=b'\wedge c<c'$. Write $\rho(d,a,b,c)$ for the corresponding element.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Divisibility]\label{lem:div}
For every $n\ge1$ and $g\in\Gamma$ there is $h\in\Gamma$ with $nh=g$; explicitly
$h=\rho(d/n,a/n,b/n,c/n)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Coordinatewise division in $\R$, using $n\cdot\rho(d,a,b,c)=\rho(nd,na,nb,nc)$, which
follows by induction from the additivity of $\rho$.
\end{proof}

The intended meaning of a rank is fixed by the dictionary
\[
\rho(d,a,b,c)\;\longleftrightarrow\;
\mm_{d,a,b,c}(x)=\exp\bigl(d\,e^{x}\bigr)\exp(ax)\,x^{b}(\log x)^{c}.
\]

\subsection{Hahn series}

\begin{definition}[Transseries]
The field of EML transseries is $\T=\R(\!(t^{\Gamma})\!)$, the field of Hahn series:
functions $f:\Gamma\to\R$ with well-ordered support, under pointwise addition and
convolution product. For $f\neq0$ set $\ord(f)=\min\supp(f)$ and $\lc(f)=f(\ord f)$, and
declare $f>0$ iff $\lc(f)>0$. This makes $\T$ a strictly ordered field.
\end{definition}

\begin{convention}[Sign of the exponent]
In a Hahn series a \emph{large} exponent means a \emph{small} series. Since a large
transmonomial should be a large transseries, we place $\mm_{d,a,b,c}$ at the rank
$\rho(-d,-a,-b,-c)$ and write
\[
T(d,a,b,c)=t^{\rho(-d,-a,-b,-c)}\in\T,\qquad C(r)=r\,t^{0}\in\T .
\]
Thus $x=T(0,0,1,0)$, $\log x=T(0,0,0,1)$, $e^{x}=T(0,1,0,0)$, $e^{e^{x}}=T(1,0,0,0)$.
\end{convention}

\subsection{Transmonomials as a group, and the comparison theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Multiplicative structure]
The map $(d,a,b,c)\mapsto T(d,a,b,c)$ is an injective group homomorphism
$(\R^{4},+)\to(\T^{\times},\cdot)$:
\[
T(d,a,b,c)T(d',a',b',c')=T(d+d',a+a',b+b',c+c'),\quad T(0,0,0,0)=1,
\]
\[
T(d,a,b,c)^{-1}=T(-d,-a,-b,-c),\quad T(d,a,b,c)^{n}=T(nd,na,nb,nc).
\]
Each $T(d,a,b,c)$ is positive with leading coefficient $1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Monomials multiply by adding exponents, $t^{g}t^{h}=t^{g+h}$, and $\rho$ is additive;
positivity is immediate from $\lc=1>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Scale Comparison Theorem]\label{thm:scale}
For all real $d,a,b,c,d',a',b',c'$,
\[
T(d,a,b,c)<T(d',a',b',c')\iff (d,a,b,c)<_{\mathrm{lex}}(d',a',b',c').
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Two positive monomials with equal coefficients compare inversely to their ranks: if
$g'<g$ then $t^{g}<t^{g'}$, since at the least index where the two series differ --- namely
$g'$ --- the second has a positive coefficient and the first has zero. Combining with the
sign convention and the fact that negation reverses the lexicographic order gives the
claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Growth hierarchy]
For every $n\in\mathbb{N}$ and $r\in\R$,
\[
C(r)<\log x,\qquad (\log x)^{n}<x,\qquad x^{n}<e^{x},\qquad (e^{x})^{n}<e^{e^{x}},
\]
and $x^{-1}<C(r)$ for every real $r>0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Non-Archimedeanity]
$\T$ is not Archimedean: $n\cdot1=C(n)<\log x<x$ for every $n$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Roots, squares and the rigidity of the ordering}

\subsection{Root extraction}

\begin{lemma}[Leading decomposition]\label{lem:leading}
Every nonzero $f\in\T$ can be written $f=t^{g}\,r\,u$ with $g=\ord(f)$, $r=\lc(f)\neq0$
and $u=1+\varepsilon$ where $\ord(\varepsilon)>0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Set $u=r^{-1}t^{-g}f$; its order is $0$ and leading coefficient $1$, so $u-1$ has strictly
positive order.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Binomial roots of $1$-units]\label{lem:binom}
For every $1$-unit $u=1+\varepsilon$ and every $n\ge1$ there is a $1$-unit $v$ with
$v^{n}=u$, namely $v=u^{1/n}$ defined by
\[
u^{s}=\sum_{k\ge0}\binom{s}{k}\varepsilon^{k},\qquad
\binom{s}{k}=\frac{s(s-1)\cdots(s-k+1)}{k!},
\]
a well-defined element of $\T$ because $\ord(\varepsilon^{k})=k\,\ord(\varepsilon)$
increases without bound, so the family is summable.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The binomial power satisfies $u^{s}u^{s'}=u^{s+s'}$, $u^{0}=1$, $u^{1}=u$; hence
$u^{ns}=(u^{s})^{n}$ by induction, and $s=1/n$ gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
This step is where infinite sums are essential:
$(1+x^{-1})^{1/2}=1+\tfrac12x^{-1}-\tfrac18x^{-2}+\tfrac1{16}x^{-3}-\cdots$ is genuinely
infinite even though $\varepsilon=x^{-1}$ is a single monomial.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Root extraction]\label{thm:roots}
If $f>0$ and $n\ge1$, there is $h>0$ with $h^{n}=f$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Decompose $f=t^{g}ru$ as in Lemma~\ref{lem:leading}, with $r>0$. Choose $g_{0}$ with
$ng_{0}=g$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:div}), $s=r^{1/n}>0$ (real closedness of $\R$), and
$v=u^{1/n}$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:binom}). Then $h=t^{g_{0}}sv$ satisfies $h^{n}=f$ and $h>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Euclidean field]\label{cor:sq}
$f$ is a square if and only if $f\ge0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Odd roots]
For odd $n$, every $f\in\T$ has an $n$-th root.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Formal reality]
$-1$ is not a sum of squares in $\T$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Order rigidity}

\begin{theorem}[Definability of the ordering]
For all $f,g\in\T$: $f\le g$ iff $g-f$ is a square. Consequently $0<f$ iff $f\neq0$ and
$f$ is a square, and for every $f$ either $f$ or $-f$ is a square.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness of the ordering]
Let $R$ be any binary relation on $\T$ such that $R(0,s^{2})$ for all $s$, $R$ is
translation invariant, and $R$ is antisymmetric. Then $R(f,g)\iff f\le g$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $f\le g$ then $g-f=s^{2}$, so $R(0,g-f)$ and hence $R(f,g)$ by translation. Conversely
if $R(f,g)$ but $g<f$ then likewise $R(g,f)$, and antisymmetry gives $f=g$, a
contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Automatic monotonicity]\label{thm:mono}
Every ring homomorphism $\varphi:\T\to K$ into an ordered field $K$ is monotone. In
particular every field automorphism of $\T$ preserves the asymptotic ordering.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $f\le g$, write $g-f=s^{2}$; then $\varphi(g)-\varphi(f)=\varphi(s)^{2}\ge0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Quadratics]
If $a\neq0$ and $b^{2}-4ac\ge0$ then $az^{2}+bz+c=0$ has two solutions in $\T$ with sum
$-b/a$. Conversely a monic quadratic $z^{2}+bz+c$ has a root in $\T$ iff $b^{2}-4c\ge0$.
In particular $\sqrt{x}\in\T$ while $z^{2}=-1$ is unsolvable.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Forwards, take $s$ with $s^{2}=b^{2}-4ac$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:sq}) and
$z=(-b\pm s)/(2a)$. Backwards, $(2z+b)^{2}=b^{2}-4c$ exhibits the discriminant as a
square.
\end{proof}

\section{The EML algebra and the faithfulness of expansion}

\begin{definition}[EML algebra]
Let $\mathcal{E}=\R[\Gamma]$ be the group algebra of finitely supported $p:\Gamma\to\R$,
with convolution product; an element is a finite formal combination $\sum_{g}p(g)[g]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Two maps]
For $p\in\mathcal{E}$ put $\Phi(p)=\sum_{g}p(g)t^{g}\in\T$ (formal expansion) and
\[
E_{p}(x)=\sum_{g}p(g)\rr_{g}(x),\qquad
\rr_{\rho(d,a,b,c)}(x)=\exp(-d\,e^{x})\exp(-ax)\,x^{-b}(\log x)^{-c}\quad(x>1),
\]
the \emph{EML function} of $p$. Both $\Phi$ and $p\mapsto[E_{p}]$ (germ at $+\infty$) are
ring homomorphisms.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Analytic meaning of the order]\label{thm:tendsto}
If $g>0$ in $\Gamma$ then $\rr_{g}(x)\to0$; if $g<0$ then $\rr_{g}(x)\to+\infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\log\rr_{g}(x)=-d e^{x}-ax-b\log x-c\log\log x$; lexicographic positivity of $(d,a,b,c)$
means the first nonzero coordinate is positive, and each term dominates all later ones
since $e^{x}\gg x\gg\log x\gg\log\log x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Dominant-term theorem]\label{thm:dom}
If $p\neq0$ has minimal support rank $g_{0}$ with $\kappa=p(g_{0})$, then
$E_{p}(x)/\rr_{g_{0}}(x)\to\kappa$. In particular $E_{p}$ is eventually of constant sign
$\sgn(\kappa)$ and eventually nonvanishing.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Divide by $\rr_{g_{0}}$: the $g_{0}$-term gives $\kappa$, and each other term gives
$p(g)\rr_{g-g_{0}}$ with $g-g_{0}>0$, tending to $0$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:tendsto}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Faithfulness]\label{thm:faith}
For $p,q\in\mathcal{E}$,
\[
E_{p}=E_{q}\text{ eventually}\iff\Phi(p)=\Phi(q),\qquad
E_{p}<E_{q}\text{ eventually}\iff\Phi(p)<\Phi(q).
\]
Both $\Phi$ and $p\mapsto[E_{p}]$ are injective with identical fibres.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By linearity reduce to $q=0$. If $r\neq0$, Theorem~\ref{thm:dom} says $E_{r}$ has the
eventual sign of $\lc(\Phi(r))$ and is eventually nonzero; this gives both directions.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The germs at $+\infty$ of EML functions form an ordered integral domain order-isomorphic
to $\Phi(\mathcal{E})\subseteq\T$ --- a Hardy field.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Asymptotic Comparison Theorem, formal]\label{thm:acf}
If $u\in\T$ satisfies $|u|<T(d,a,b,c)$ for all real $d,a,b,c$, then $u=0$. Equivalently,
$f=h$ iff $|f-h|<T(d,a,b,c)$ for all $d,a,b,c$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $u\neq0$, put $g_{1}=\ord(|u|)$. Any monomial of rank strictly larger than $g_{1}$ is
strictly smaller than $|u|$; taking $g=g_{1}+\rho(0,0,0,1)$ contradicts the hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No flat germs]\label{thm:flat}
If $p\neq0$ then there is a rank $g$ with $E_{p}\neq o(\rr_{g})$; indeed $g=g_{0}$ works,
since $E_{p}\sim\kappa\rr_{g_{0}}$ with $\kappa\neq0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Asymptotic Comparison Theorem, analytic]
If $E_{p}-E_{q}=o(\rr_{g})$ for every rank $g$, then $p=q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:flat} to $p-q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
This is exactly the failure of the flat-function pathology: the EML scale contains no
analogue of $e^{-1/x^{2}}$. Every nonzero EML function is seen by the scale, so the
transseries expansion is a complete asymptotic invariant.
\end{remark}

\section{Differential structure}

Differentiating $\rr_{\rho(d,a,b,c)}=\exp(-de^{x}-ax-b\log x-c\log\log x)$ gives
\[
\frac{\rr_{g}'}{\rr_{g}}=-d\,e^{x}-a-\frac{b}{x}-\frac{c}{x\log x},
\]
and $e^{x},1,1/x,1/(x\log x)$ are again transmonomials, of ranks $\rho(0,-1,0,0)$, $0$,
$\rho(0,0,1,0)$, $\rho(0,0,1,1)$.

\begin{definition}
Let $\dlog(g)=-d[\rho(0,-1,0,0)]-a[0]-b[\rho(0,0,1,0)]-c[\rho(0,0,1,1)]$ and
$D(p)=\sum_{g}\bigl(p(g)[g]\bigr)\cdot\dlog(g)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Leibniz rule]
$D$ is additive and $D(pq)=D(p)q+pD(q)$; thus $(\mathcal{E},D)$ is a differential ring.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both sides are additive, so it suffices to check on monomials, where the identity reduces
to $\dlog(g+h)=\dlog(g)+\dlog(h)$, i.e.\ additivity of the coordinate functionals.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The formal derivation is the analytic one]\label{thm:deriv}
For every $p\in\mathcal{E}$ and every $x>1$, $E_{p}$ is differentiable at $x$ with
$E_{p}'(x)=E_{D(p)}(x)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Constants]
$D(p)=0$ iff $p=c\,[0]$ for some $c\in\R$, iff $E_{p}$ is eventually constant.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $D(p)=0$ then $E_{p}'\equiv0$ on $(1,\infty)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:deriv}, so
$E_{p}\equiv c$ by the mean value theorem; then $q=p-c[0]$ has $E_{q}\equiv0$, and
injectivity (Theorem~\ref{thm:faith}) gives $q=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Hardy-field behaviour]
For every $p\in\mathcal{E}$: (i) exactly one of $E_{p}\to L\in\R$, $E_{p}\to+\infty$,
$E_{p}\to-\infty$ holds; (ii) there is $N$ with $E_{p}$ strictly increasing, strictly
decreasing, or constant on $[N,\infty)$; (iii) if $p$ is not constant, $E_{p}$ is
injective on some $[N,\infty)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(i) is Theorem~\ref{thm:dom} together with Theorem~\ref{thm:tendsto} applied to
$g_{0}\gtrless0$. For (ii), $D(p)\in\mathcal{E}$, so $E_{p}'=E_{D(p)}$ has eventually
constant sign, or vanishes identically; a continuous function with constant-sign
derivative on a half-line is strictly monotone there. (iii) follows from (ii).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Liouville-type obstruction]
$1/x$ has the antiderivative $\log x$ in $\mathcal{E}$, but $1/(x\log x)$ has none:
there is no $p\in\mathcal{E}$ with $D(p)=[\rho(0,0,1,1)]$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
First, $\log\log x$ is flat against the whole growing part of the scale: if
$\rr_{g}\to+\infty$ then $g<0$, and the slowest such growth is $(\log x)^{c}$ with $c>0$,
against which $\log\log x=o((\log x)^{c})$. Hence no $E_{p}$ equals $\log\log x+K$
eventually, since such an $E_{p}\to+\infty$ would be asymptotic to $\kappa\rr_{g_{0}}$
with $\kappa\neq0$ and $\rr_{g_0}\to+\infty$. Finally if $D(p)=[\rho(0,0,1,1)]$ then
$E_{p}(x)-\log\log x$ has zero derivative on $(1,\infty)$, hence is a constant $K$,
contradicting the previous sentence.
\end{proof}

\section{Towards real closedness}

A field is \emph{real closed} if it is semireal, every element or its negative is a
square, and every odd-degree polynomial has a root. For $\T$ the first two hold by
Section~3.

\begin{theorem}[Reduction]\label{thm:red}
$\T$ is real closed iff every monic odd-degree $P\in\T[z]$ has a root in $\T$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The residue field is real closed]
$\R$ is real closed: nonnegative reals are squares, and an odd-degree real polynomial
changes sign at $\pm\infty$, hence has a root by the intermediate value theorem.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Roots beyond radicals}

\begin{definition}
$t\in\T$ is \emph{small} (infinitesimal) if $|t|<C(r)$ for every real $r>0$. Every
$T(d,a,b,c)$ with $(d,a,b,c)<_{\mathrm{lex}}0$ is small; e.g.\ $1/x$, $1/\log x$,
$e^{-x}$. Small elements have strictly positive order.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Henselianity of $\R[[X]]$]\label{thm:hensel}
$\R[[X]]$ is $X$-adically complete, hence Henselian at $(X)$. Consequently, if
$F\in\R[[X]][z]$ is monic and its residue $F_{0}=F\bmod X\in\R[z]$ has a simple real root
$a$ (i.e.\ $F_{0}(a)=0$, $F_{0}'(a)\neq0$), then $F$ has a root $y\in\R[[X]]$ with
constant term $a$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Completeness is immediate from the definition of a formal power series; a complete local
ring is Henselian. Concretely, Newton's iteration $y_{k+1}=y_{k}-F(y_{k})/F'(y_{k})$
converges $X$-adically, $F'(y_{k})$ being a unit since its constant term $F_{0}'(a)\neq0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Substituting an infinitesimal]
For small $t\in\T$ there is a ring homomorphism $\mathrm{ev}_{t}:\R[[X]]\to\T$ with
$\mathrm{ev}_{t}(X)=t$, $\mathrm{ev}_{t}(r)=C(r)$, and rank-$0$ coefficient of
$\mathrm{ev}_{t}(f)$ equal to $f(0)$. It sends roots to roots.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $\ord(t)>0$, the family $(f_{k}t^{k})_{k}$ is summable, so $f\mapsto\sum_{k}f_{k}t^{k}$
is well defined and multiplicative.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Deformation]
If $F\in\R[[X]][z]$ is monic and its specialisation at $X=0$ has a simple real root, then
for every small $t\in\T$ the polynomial obtained by substituting $t$ for $X$ has a root in
$\T$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Casus irreducibilis]
For every small $t\in\T$ the cubic $z^{3}-3z+t=0$ has a root in $\T$, although its Cardano
discriminant is strictly negative:
\[
\left(\frac{t}{2}\right)^{2}+\left(\frac{-3}{3}\right)^{3}=\frac{t^{2}}{4}-1<0 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The residue cubic $z^{3}-3z$ has the simple root $0$ (derivative $-3$ there), so the
deformation theorem applies; and $|t|<C(2)$ gives $t^{2}<4$.
\end{proof}

Since a cubic with negative Cardano discriminant is not solvable by real radicals, this
root lies outside the reach of Theorem~\ref{thm:roots}. In contrast, every cubic with
nonnegative discriminant is solved outright by Cardano's formula, using only the square
and cube roots supplied by Theorem~\ref{thm:roots}.

\subsection{Newton scaling}

\begin{definition}
For $\lambda\in\T^{\times}$ and monic $P\in\T[z]$ of degree $n$, put
$\mathcal{N}_{\lambda}(P)(z)=\lambda^{-n}P(\lambda z)$. It is monic of degree $n$, has
$i$-th coefficient $\lambda^{i-n}a_{i}$, and $z$ is its root iff $\lambda z$ is a root of
$P$. Call a monic $P$ \emph{normalised} if $|a_{i}|\le1$ for all $i$ and, unless
$P=z^{n}$, $|a_{i}|=1$ for some $i<n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Newton Normalisation Theorem]\label{thm:newton}
For every monic $P\in\T[z]$ there is $\lambda>0$ in $\T$ with $\mathcal{N}_{\lambda}(P)$
normalised; explicitly
\[
\lambda=\max_{\substack{i<n\\ a_{i}\neq0}}|a_{i}|^{1/(n-i)},
\]
with $\lambda=1$ if $P=z^{n}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The maximum exists because the order is total and the index set finite; the fractional
powers exist by Theorem~\ref{thm:roots}. Then $\lambda^{n-i}\ge|a_{i}|$ for all $i<n$
gives $|a_{i}\lambda^{i-n}|\le1$, and the maximising index gives equality.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cauchy root bound]
If $P$ is monic with all $|a_{i}|\le1$ and $P(z)=0$, then $|z|<2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $|z|\ge2$ then
$|z|^{n}\le\sum_{i<n}|z|^{i}=\frac{|z|^{n}-1}{|z|-1}\le|z|^{n}-1$, a contradiction. The
computation is valid in any ordered field.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpened reduction]\label{thm:sharp}
$\T$ is real closed if and only if every \emph{normalised} monic odd-degree polynomial
over $\T$ has a root.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Given monic odd-degree $P$, normalise by Theorem~\ref{thm:newton}; a root $z$ of the
normalised polynomial yields the root $\lambda z$ of $P$. Conclude with
Theorem~\ref{thm:red}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The remaining gap}

Full real closedness of $\T$ is \emph{not} established. Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} reduces it
to exactly the situation in which the Newton-polygon/Hensel argument operates: monic of
odd degree $n$, all coefficients in the valuation ring, residue polynomial a genuine monic
real polynomial of degree $n$ different from $z^{n}$, residue field real closed, value
group divisible, all roots bounded by $2$. The remaining difficulty is the case of a
\emph{multiple} residue root: Theorem~\ref{thm:hensel} lifts only simple residue roots, and
a multiple root requires a further Newton-polygon induction on the multiplicity, whose
well-foundedness over the non-discrete value group $\R^{4}$ is the crux.

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{description}
\item[A. Dominant term and limits.] Sort ranks lexicographically, take the minimum
  $g_{0}=\rho(d,a,b,c)$ with coefficient $\kappa$. The limit is $0$ if $g_{0}>0$, $\kappa$
  if $g_{0}=0$, and $\sgn(\kappa)\infty$ if $g_{0}<0$. Cost $O(m)$ for $m$ terms.
\item[B. Roots.] Extract $(g,r,\varepsilon)$ with $f=t^{g}r(1+\varepsilon)$; output
  $t^{g/n}r^{1/n}\sum_{k\le N}\binom{1/n}{k}\varepsilon^{k}$, truncated at rank depth $N$.
\item[C. Newton scaling.] Compute $\lambda=\max_{i<n,a_{i}\neq0}|a_{i}|^{1/(n-i)}$ using B
  and lexicographic comparison; return $(a_{i}\lambda^{i-n})_{i}$.
\item[D. Hensel lifting.] Iterate $y_{k+1}=y_{k}-F(y_{k})/F'(y_{k})$ in $\R[[X]]$
  truncated at order $2^{k}$; quadratic convergence gives the root mod $X^{N}$ in
  $\lceil\log_{2}N\rceil$ steps. Substitute a small transseries for $X$.
\item[E. Differentiation.] Replace $\kappa[g]$ by $\kappa[g]\cdot\dlog(g)$ and collect;
  cost $O(m)$.
\end{description}

\section{Applications and discussion}

\textbf{Automated asymptotics.} Algorithm A is the theoretical basis of ``limit at
infinity'' facilities for exp-log expressions; Theorem~\ref{thm:faith} is what makes such
an algorithm correct, since comparing expansions decides comparison of germs.

\textbf{Resurgence.} Divergent perturbation series are completed by exponentially small
terms $e^{-S/g}$; a transseries field is the natural home for such completed expansions,
and Theorem~\ref{thm:acf} says such a field has no invisible content.

\textbf{Hardy fields and model theory.} The eventual monotonicity and limit theorems
exhibit EML germs as a Hardy field, obtained here constructively from the dominant-term
theorem alone.

\textbf{Differential algebra.} The constants theorem and the Liouville-type obstruction
place $\mathcal{E}$ in the Liouville theory of elementary integration: a differential ring
whose constants are exactly the ground field and which is not closed under integration,
the first obstruction appearing one logarithm above the classical one.

\textbf{Limitations.} The scale is four-dimensional and therefore closed under neither
exponentiation nor integration. This is deliberate: it keeps everything explicit while
still exhibiting the structural phenomena. Extension to the full exp-log hierarchy replaces
$\R^{4}$ by a direct limit of such groups.

\section{Future work}

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item \textbf{Closing the real-closedness gap.} Handle a multiple residue root by
  substituting $z\mapsto\bar a+z_{1}$ and rescaling again, showing that multiplicity
  strictly decreases; the crux is well-foundedness over a non-discrete value group.
\item \textbf{Deeper scales.} Replace $\Gamma=\R^{4}$ by a direct limit over iterated
  exponentials and logarithms, obtaining a scale closed under $\exp$, $\log$ and
  integration; the flatness argument becomes a diagonal argument.
\item \textbf{Effective transseries arithmetic.} Assemble Algorithms A--E into a certified
  library for exp-log limits, expansions and root finding, with the theorems above as
  correctness statements.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

Recording a growth rate as a point of $\R^{4}$ and comparing points lexicographically turns
the informal hierarchy $e^{e^{x}}\gg e^{x}\gg x\gg\log x$ into arithmetic. The Hahn series
construction then supplies an ordered field in which infinitely large and infinitely small
quantities are first-class citizens; root extraction makes it Euclidean, hence its ordering
definable, unique and rigid; and dominant-term analysis makes the expansion map faithful,
so that a function and its expansion are interchangeable. The Asymptotic Comparison
Theorem --- no nonzero object hides below the whole scale --- is the technical heart, and it
is what rescues asymptotic expansion from the flat-function pathology that afflicts power
series. Real closedness has been reduced to a single sharply stated clause about normalised
monic odd-degree polynomials, with all the classical Newton-polygon inputs verified.

\end{document}

